---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="Meet the computer science teacher behind LogicDash: daily binary puzzles, approachable computer science lessons and free classroom tools."
>
	<section class="hero">
		<h1>About</h1>
		<p class="hero-lead">A little logic, a lot of curiosity.</p>
	</section>

	<div class="blocks">
		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">The Story</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				I'm a computer science teacher, and LogicDash started with a simple observation: logic
				is at the heart of programming, maths and problem-solving, but practising it can feel
				a bit dry. I wanted to make something people would genuinely enjoy coming back to.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				The first idea was a binary guessing game. You don't get told which digits are wrong,
				only how many. It looks simple, but working out the best next guess takes real thought.
				That small puzzle has gradually grown into something broader.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">Puzzle / Learn / Tools</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				<strong>Puzzles:</strong> <a href="/quick-byte/">Quick Byte</a> is the main event:
				three five-bit puzzles each day, with the same challenge for everyone.
				For unlimited practice there's <a href="/5-bit/">Classic</a>, or
				<a href="/overclocked/">OverClocked</a> for a tougher six-bit challenge.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				<strong>Learn:</strong> <a href="/learn/">Byte Lab</a> has short, approachable
				computer science lessons and hands-on practice, from binary and algorithms to
				networks and security.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				<strong>Tools:</strong> <a href="/tools/">The Tool Hut</a> holds practical,
				free-to-use classroom helpers — a countdown clock, seating charts,
				a student picker and a binary clock.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">Built for Curious Minds</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				You don't need to be a programmer to enjoy LogicDash. Some visitors come for a quick
				brain workout; others are learning their first binary number, studying computing or
				looking for something useful in the classroom. I want the site to work for all of them.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				I've also run <a href="https://funkyenglish.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FunkyEnglish</a>,
				a website for English language learners, for many years. If you're studying computing
				in English, its <a href="https://funkyenglish.com/computer-science-vocabulary-part-1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Computer Science Vocabulary</a>
				lesson may be useful alongside Byte Lab.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">Still a Work in Progress</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				LogicDash is an independent project, built and maintained by one person in the time
				I can find outside teaching. I'm continuing to refine the puzzles, expand the lessons
				and improve the tools. If you've spotted something that could be better — or have a
				good idea — <a href="/contact/">I'd like to hear it.</a>
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.hero {
		text-align: center;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}

	.hero h1 {
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 5vw, 2.1rem);
	}

	.hero-lead {
		margin: 0 auto;
		max-width: 34rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

	.blocks {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.75rem;
	}

	.block {
		background: var(--color-surface);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		border-radius: 14px;
		padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
	}

	.block-title {
		margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		color: #ffae2e;
	}

	.block-text {
		margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

	.block-text:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.block-text strong { color: var(--color-text); }

	.block-text a {
		color: #ffae2e;
		font-weight: 600;
	}
</style>
